³1ip³2217.247.143.203³2ts³21095292416³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3continued development³3text³3= Where can I get Linux =
= and =
= what is the difference between all these different types of Linux? =

Part One: Where can I get Linux?

You can get Linux from the net. Download it for free. You can get Linux from a friend. Make a copy of it. Or you can buy the distribution CD(s) on the net or at stores.

Since most all this software is 'free' you can legally copy and pass it on. The copy you get from the net is free and legal. The copy you get from a friend is legal. And any copies you make for friends (or anyone) are legal. That is part of the reason for Open Software.

The smallest distributions tend to fill a CD. The larger distributions can be eight or more CDs.  Thus downloading a complete copy takes a bit of time. Many people prefer to buy the CDs (figure $2 to $15 depeding on the version and number of CDs in that version).

There are generally two types of distribution-- standard and live CD. The standard is only meant for installation on your hard drive and the live CDs also run directly from bootable CD-ROM drives without disturbing anything on the hard drive.

You can go straight to the distribution producers, for  example:
		
#Live CD Distributions- No install required, these let you try Linux without having to format or partition a hard drive.
##[http://www.knoppix.org/ Knoppix]
##[http://www.gnoppix.org/ Gnoppix]
##[http://www.damnsmalllinux.org/ Damn Small Linux] 
##[http://www.mandrakelinux.com/en/mandrakemove/ Mandrake Move] 
#Standard Distributions
##[http://www.debian.org/ Debian GNU/Linux]
##[http://www.redhat.com/fedora/ The Fedora Project (formerly Redhat Linux)]
##[http://www.mandrakelinux.com/ Mandrake Linux] 
##[http://www.suse.com Suse Linux] 

Or you can order from distribution suppliers, for example:

[http://xplinux.biz/shop/ The Linux CD Shop]

